[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Emergency Medicine Physician (Lead)): Specializes in acute care and urgent diagnosis, this member will assess the patient's immediate condition, perform a physical examination, and manage acute symptoms such as fever and respiratory status.
 Member 2 (Pediatric Infectious Disease Specialist): Focuses on diagnosing and managing infections in children and adolescents, particularly to evaluate for possible sepsis and infectious causes such as toxic shock syndrome or meningitis.
 Member 3 (Gynecologist): Evaluates the patient for possible complications related to menstruation, including excessive bleeding and any relation to retained tampons that could lead to toxic shock syndrome.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Clinical Laboratory Scientist (Lead)): Manages laboratory testing including blood cultures, CBC (complete blood count), and coagulation studies critical for diagnosing underlying infectious or hematological issues.
 Member 2 (Radiologist): Provides imaging support, interpreting necessary scans to rule out any intracranial complications (e.g., abscess, hemorrhage) and assessing the extent of any potential complications associated with the presented symptoms.
 Member 3 (Immunologist): Focuses on evaluating the immune response and lymphocyte activation, which is crucial in diagnosing conditions related to bacterial infections and T cell receptor involvement in the patient’s presentation.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Pediatric Consultant (Lead)): Provides overall clinical expertise, ensuring the integration of information and recommendations from all specialties to develop a cohesive management plan.
 Member 2 (Clinical Decision Support Specialist): Coordinates communication among team members, analyzing the input from the IAT and DET to formulate a final treatment strategy based on the most likely diagnosis and urgent intervention needed.
 Member 3 (Ethics Consultant): Advises on any ethical concerns that may arise regarding the patient's care, especially in considering the treatment options, implications of her age, and informed consent processes in the context of her acute condition.


