[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Pediatric Hematologist (Lead)): Specializes in blood disorders and is critical in diagnosing causes of anemia and thrombocytopenia. This member will lead the evaluation and initial management of the child’s hematologic condition.
 Member 2 (Pediatric Infectious Disease Specialist): Provides expertise in evaluating and managing infections that could contribute to the child’s symptoms, including recent travel
 Member 3 (Pediatric Nephrologist): Assesses kidney function given the elevated creatinine and urea nitrogen levels and evaluates for potential acute kidney injury as part of the hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) differential.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Pediatric Pathologist (Lead)): Reviews laboratory results and blood smears to confirm the presence of schistocytes and assist in diagnosing conditions like Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP) or Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (HUS).
 Member 2 (Pediatric Gastroenterologist): Investigates if the child's prior abdominal symptoms and bloody diarrhea could indicate an infectious etiology or gastrointestinal involvement related to hemolytic anemia.
 Member 3 (Radiologist (Pediatric Specialist)): Evaluates the need for imaging studies to assess for possible renal involvement or other organ damage related to possible TTP or HUS.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Pediatric Consultant (Lead)): Oversees the entire case, synthesizes information from all teams, and provides expert guidance regarding the most likely diagnosis and treatment approach.
 Member 2 (Clinical Pharmacologist): Evaluates the appropriateness of potential therapies that might be required for the confirmed diagnosis (e.g., plasma exchange in TTP, supportive care in HUS).
 Member 3 (Clinical Decision Specialist): Coordinates between different specialty teams, integrating their findings into a cohesive plan and ensuring the management decisions align with the child’s overall best interests.


